TA-disk
====

Collects disk health from smartctl (SATA/ATA SMART + NVMe) and /sys/class/nvme: health status, power-on hours, temperature, NVMe wear (percentage used, available spare), media errors and SMART raw counters (reallocated, uncorrected, CRC). Emits per-disk JSON events to index=main (sourcetype disk:status) and numeric disk.<device>.<attribute> metrics to index=metrics (sourcetype disk:metrics), with overview and metrics dashboards.

Prerequisites
-------------
  * smartmontools (smartctl) installed on the host
  * nvme disks are read from /sys/class/nvme, no nvme-cli required
  * Splunk Enterprise or Universal Forwarder 9.x - 10.6 (forwarder recommended
    on monitored hosts).
  * A metrics-typed index (default name: metrics) on the indexer:

      [metrics]
      datatype = metric

    Forwarders only need that index to exist on the indexer side.
  * No network egress. Fully standalone add-on with no dependencies.
  * SMART access: if the Splunk service runs as root, smartctl works out of the
    box. If it runs as an unprivileged user (e.g. "splunk"), see SMART access
    below - per-disk telemetry (temperature, wear, raw counters) otherwise
    stays empty (smart_available=0).

Install
-------
Install on each monitored host (Apps > Install app from file, splunk install
app, or deployment server), restart Splunk, then verify:

  index=main sourcetype="disk:status" | head

Search quick reference
----------------------
  index=main sourcetype="disk:status" | head
  | mcatalog values(metric_name) where index=metrics metric_name="disk.*" by host
  | mstats avg("disk.*") where index=metrics span=60s by host

Usage
-----
Events: index=main sourcetype=disk:status  (per-disk JSON: model, serial, firmware,
health, temperature, power_on_hours, percentage_used, available_spare, media_errors, raw counters)
Metrics: disk.<device>_<attribute> on index=metrics
Dashboards: TA-Disk > Disk Overview / Disk Metrics

Configure
---------
Override the interval or destination index in etc/apps/TA-disk/local/inputs.conf
(never edit default/).

SMART access (non-root forwarder service)
------------------------------------------
smartctl needs root for NVMe/ATA health ioctls. When splunkd runs as an
unprivileged user, grant it passwordless smartctl and point the inputs at a
sudo wrapper (helper files ship in deploy/ inside this app):

  1. Install the wrapper (root):
       install -m 0755 deploy/ta_disk_smartctl /usr/local/sbin/ta_disk_smartctl
  2. Install the sudoers rule (adjust user and the smartctl path -
     command -v smartctl - then validate with visudo):
       cp deploy/ta_disk.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/ta-disk && chmod 0440 ... && visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/ta-disk
  3. Restart Splunk - the add-on auto-detects /usr/local/sbin/ta_disk_smartctl
     and prefers it when present (falls back to smartctl otherwise; an
     explicit TA_DISK_SMARTCTL process environment variable overrides all).
  4. Verify:
       sudo -u splunk /usr/local/sbin/ta_disk_smartctl -j -a /dev/nvme0n1
     should print an nvme_smart_health_information_log, and
       | mcatalog values(metric_name) where index=metrics metric_name="disk.*percentage_used"

Troubleshooting
---------------
  * If no data: verify smartctl is installed and the Splunk service can run it (splunkd runs as root or the splunk user; SMART needs read access to /dev/block devices).
  * Read-only filesystem or non-SMART disks (virtio) are skipped gracefully.
  * NVMe wear fields exist only on NVMe devices; SATA disks emit SMART raw counters instead.

License
-------
SOURCE-AVAILABLE (see LICENSE) - free personal, educational and evaluation
use; commercial or production use requires a paid license from BentleyPC
LLC (https://www.bentleypc.com/TA-disk).

Enter your key (format BP-...) after purchase using EITHER method:

  1. Splunk Web: open the app, click "License" in the nav, click a row to
     open the key entry page, paste the key and Save.

  2. File (headless / forwarder friendly): create the file
     $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/apps/TA-disk/local/license.conf with:

         [settings]
         telemetry_enabled = 1
         key = BP-xxxxxxxxxxxx

     No restart required - the hourly beacon reads this file on every run.

Set telemetry_enabled = 0 to keep the key for validation but pause all
outbound beacons. The beacon sends only: license key, hostname, app name
and app version to the BentleyPC license API (see api_base in
default/license.conf). It is telemetry only - never a license check that
blocks functionality.
